      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "A Declaration exposes one repeatable surface port; each Surface is a View chain ending in a Glyph; and the Declaration resolves views like any class or template node",
        "body": "**Progress, 2026-09-24:** tasks 1 to 6 are built and tested; 8, 9, 10, 11 and 12 are partly done (each task says what is and is not); 7 is not started and waits for the Declaration template (question 8, now answered). `npm test` is 1022/1022.\n\nThe target model is written down in `primitives/declaration/root.node` (mikemartinez1974/public): the Declaration has ONE repeatable `surface` port; each edge from it points at the first View of a Surface; a Surface is a chain View → View → … → Glyph, so every Surface has its own glyph; and a Declaration takes part in view resolution exactly as a class or template node does, with no special path. That supersedes five relationships (`default-view`, `summary-view`, `icon-view`, `view-sequence` and the standalone `glyph`), which stay readable as legacy.\n\n**What was true when this was written (before tasks 1 to 6; verified by reading the code, not by running it):**\n\n1. **Grammar.** `components/GraphEditor/utils/declarationRelationshipHandles.js` defines ten relationships. `default-view`/`summary-view`/`icon-view` come from `PORT_SEMANTIC_RELATIONSHIPS.slice(0, 3)` in `portRelationshipHandles.js`, which Port nodes also use. `view-sequence` and `glyph` are separate entries. There is no `surface` relationship.\n2. **Edge classes** (content repo, `mikemartinez1974/public/classes/edges/`, read by the app from `DEFAULT_EDGE_CLASSES_REF`). `view-sequence` is declaration|view → view with `maxOutgoing 1`, so the Declaration can point at only one head and a chain cannot end in a Glyph. `shared-glyph` is declaration|port → glyph, max 1. A Glyph node has only a `root` input port (`createDefaultGlyphNode`), and Views get `sequence-in`/`sequence-out` from `createViewSequencePorts` (`portDeclarations.js`).\n3. **Chain walking.** `utils/viewSequence.js` `collectViewSequence` follows one head chain, views only, then appends the role-wired default/summary/icon views. No glyph, one chain.\n4. **Graph-level ladder.** `utils/graphViewPresentation.js` `collectGraphViewBands` scans EVERY node in the graph for Views: one graph-wide ladder, not per chain.\n5. **Glyph resolution is done three separate ways**, none chain-aware: `graphNodeContract.js` `getSharedGlyph`, the glyph fallbacks inside `resolvePercentageGraphView` (`declaration.data.glyph`, else the first glyph node anywhere in the graph), and `WorkspaceLayer.js` `resolveWorkspaceGraphIdentity`.\n6. **Validator.** `validators/validateGraphInvariants.js` `requiredRootRoles` warns `DECLARATION_ROOT_RELATIONSHIP_MISSING` unless an implicit-root Declaration has default-view, summary-view, icon-view, glyph and landing-surface. A Declaration authored in the new model would warn on all four.\n7. **Defaults.** `createDefaultGraph` (`utils/systemNodeDefaults.js`) wires default/summary/icon role edges plus a landing surface.\n8. **The Declaration is special-cased.** `nodeTypeRegistry.js` registers `declaration` with `component: ManifestNode`, and `GraphEditorContent.js` `resolveNodeComponent` returns null for a registered type with no class binding (`nodeTypes?.[node.type] && !hasClassBinding`). Class and template nodes instead go through `resolveNodeClassContract` → `viewDefinitions` → `selectViewAtResolution`. The Declaration never reaches that path.\n9. **Authoring UI** keys off the old handle ids: `GraphEditor.js` handle-drag creation (`startHandleKey === 'summary-view'` etc.), the declaration view-binding fields in `PropertiesPanel.js` and `DynamicViewNode.js`, and label/role lists in `FixedNode.js`, `PortNode.js`, `nodeVisibilityRoles.js`.\n10. **`declaration.surfaces[]` / `defaultSurfaceId`** exist as data (`{id, kind, label, portNodeId, viewNodeId, exposes}`) but are used for bridge export selection (`selectDeclarationSurface` in `creationVocabulary.js`), not presentation.\n\n**Decisions (2026-09-24, from review with the owner and a second review by Codex):**\n- **Every Surface begins with a View.** A graph that wants to show as just a glyph authors an icon View that renders a glyph, and ends its chain with its ordinary Glyph. There is no glyph-only Surface and no `surface` edge to a Glyph.\n- **Surface identity has one authority: the `surface` edge.** It carries a stable `data.surfaceId`, and chain membership decides which Views belong. Rename = change that one field. Delete = remove the edge (its Views stay in the graph, in no Surface). Duplicate ids: the first edge in graph order wins, later ones are ignored with a warning. `defaultSurfaceId` must name an existing Surface, else the first Surface is the default (warning). `declaration.surfaces[]` entries of `kind:'export'` are untouched; entries of kind view/port are read as legacy and never written for presentation.\n- **A Surface picks what renders inside the graph's frame; it never changes the frame.** Changing Surface, resolution or focus must not resize the frame, reflow neighbors or move the camera.\n- **Legacy relationships are preserved, not remapped.** The old default-view/summary-view/icon-view/view-sequence/glyph relationships keep working exactly as written; a compatibility resolver synthesizes ONE default Surface from them. They are never turned into three Surfaces.\n- **Two presentations, two owners.** The Surfaces on a Declaration describe how the GRAPH presents (embedded, opened). How the Declaration node presents itself on a canvas is a separate question, answered by its own default View through the same resolver. A Declaration is never rendered by the chains it exposes, so there is no recursion. The Declaration node's own presentation comes from a Declaration template graph through the ordinary class binding, like any class or template node (question 8).\n- **Host context is not host capability.** An explicit Surface request wins; a host's context only picks a default; capability (can this host render HTML, 3D) filters out Surfaces it cannot render.\n- **Malformed chains:** visible fallback plus diagnostics, and the resolver itself is deterministic for branches, cycles and a glyph mid-chain, because imported files do not pass through the editor's edge-class rules.\n\n**Constraints for whoever implements this:**\n- Do not grow `GraphEditorContent.js` (about 9.7k lines, excluded from lint). New logic goes in small pure modules under `utils/` with tests, then gets wired in (`utils/viewChrome.js` + `tests/viewChrome.test.js` is the pattern).\n- Existing graphs must keep working. About 131 graphs in `public/` use `summary-view` and 146 use `icon-view`. AGENTS.md's rewrite rule applies: never bulk-normalize by shape; preserve graphId, nodeId and addresses.\n- The edge class graphs are in the CONTENT repo, not this one, and the app fetches them from github at startup. Changing them is a separate publish, and the code has to tolerate the old classes until it lands.\n- Baseline was `npm test` 988/988 at authoring; it is 1022/1022 after tasks 1 to 6, 8 and 11.\n- Ports keep `PORT_SEMANTIC_RELATIONSHIPS` exactly as is (question 4). The Declaration stops reading the shared list without altering what Ports get.",
        "status": "open",
        "owner": "",
        "priority": "high",
        "estimateHours": "",
        "acceptanceCriteria": [
          "Choosing a Surface, a resolution or a focus never changes a graph frame size, moves neighbors or moves the camera",
          "A Declaration can carry any number of Surfaces through one `surface` port; each Surface is a View chain that ends in a Glyph; the app renders, validates and authors it",
          "Graphs written in the old default-view/summary-view/icon-view/glyph shape still load and render as they do today, as one default Surface",
          "The Declaration node resolves its own presentation through the same resolver as class and template nodes, with its identity card as an ordinary default View",
          "The primitives/declaration graph, the authoritative contract docs, AGENTS.md and OnboardLLM.md all describe the shipped behavior"
        ],
