      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "4. One glyph resolver: the end of the chain",
        "body": "Today three places each decide which glyph a graph shows: `graphNodeContract.js` `getSharedGlyph` / `resolveGraphPortGlyph`, the glyph fallbacks inside `resolvePercentageGraphView` in `utils/graphViewPresentation.js` (they take `declaration.data.glyph`, else the FIRST `glyph` node anywhere in the graph, which is wrong once a graph has several), and `resolveWorkspaceGraphIdentity` in `components/WorkspaceLayer.js`.\n\nReplace all three with one function, `resolveSurfaceGlyph(graph, surfaceId)`, that returns the terminal Glyph of that Surface's chain, then `declaration.data.glyph`, then the generic mark. Port-level glyph resolution (`resolveGraphPortGlyph`) keeps its port-owned lookup first, then falls through to this.\n\nDepends on: task 3.\n\n**Built (2026-09-24):** One resolver, `resolveSurfaceGlyph` / `resolveSurfaceGlyphNode` in `surfaceChains.js`: a Surface answers with the Glyph that ends its chain, then the Declaration's own glyph. All three old scanners now call it (`getSharedGlyph` in `graphNodeContract.js`, both glyph fallbacks in `resolvePercentageGraphView`, and `resolveWorkspaceGraphIdentity` in `WorkspaceLayer.js`). A legacy graph keeps its old order, and searching the whole graph for any glyph is now opt-in (`{ scan: true }`), used only by the two callers that did it before. One deliberate change: a legacy graph with several glyph nodes now prefers the glyph its Declaration wires over the first one in the file, which was the ambiguity this task was about. Compared against the old logic over all 546 Declaration graphs in `twilite/public`: zero differences.",
        "status": "done",
        "owner": "",
        "priority": "high",
        "estimateHours": "",
        "acceptanceCriteria": [
          "Exactly one function decides which glyph a Surface shows; the three old scanners call it or are removed",
          "A graph with two Surfaces and two different glyphs shows the right glyph for each",
          "A graph with a single glyph node and no chain (legacy) shows the same glyph as before",
          "tests/glyphDefinition.test.js, tests/graphViewPresentation.test.js and tests/hitboxGlyph.test.js green"
        ],
