      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "5. Scope the resolution ladder to a Surface",
        "body": "`collectGraphViewBands` in `utils/graphViewPresentation.js` takes every node in the graph that looks like a View and builds one ladder. Change it to build the ladder from ONE chain (`collectSurfaceChains`), so a second Surface's Views never mix into the first's decision. Thread an optional `surfaceId` through `resolvePercentageGraphView` and its callers in `utils/graphWorkspace.js` (`presentWorkspaceInstances`, `hasNumericViews`) and `utils/workspaceCamera.js`; the default is `declaration.defaultSurfaceId`, else the first Surface.\n\nDo NOT change the resolution math: `viewResolution.js` (`authoredViewResolution`, `selectViewAtResolution`, `occupancyResolution`) is already continuous and unbounded. Only which Views are candidates changes. A host that asks for a Surface the graph does not have falls back to the default Surface.\n\nA Surface chooses what renders INSIDE the graph's frame and nothing else: it must never resize the frame, reflow neighbors or move the camera (the workspace reflow, focus and camera work of 2026-09-22 to 24 depends on that).\n\nDepends on: task 3. A host names the Surface it wants explicitly; its context only picks a default; capability filters (decided, see goal).\n\n**Built (2026-09-24):** `collectGraphViewBands` builds the ladder from the chosen Surface's chain when the graph draws Surfaces, and from the whole graph exactly as before when it does not. `resolvePercentageGraphView`, `resolveGraphBackgroundPresentation` and `resolveGraphViewForLevel` take `{ surfaceId, hosts }`; an unknown Surface falls back to the default; below every rung the Surface's own Glyph is shown. `resolveGraphViewForLevel` was a FOURTH glyph scanner this task graph had missed (first glyph node anywhere, first View at a level anywhere) and is now scoped the same way. The workspace identifies itself as a web host (`WEB_CANVAS_HOST`, passed from `presentWorkspaceInstances` and `WorkspaceLayer.js`), so it never draws a Surface only a 3D host can. The resolution math is untouched.\n\nTested (`tests/surfaceChains.test.js`): a graph with a second, 3D-only Surface has identical card frames at five zoom levels, identical reflow with a neighbor, and an identical camera target (`resolveWorkspaceCamera`) to the same graph without it. Compared old and new level lookup and ladder across all 178 Declaration graphs in `twilite/public`: zero differences.",
        "status": "done",
        "owner": "",
        "priority": "high",
        "estimateHours": "",
        "acceptanceCriteria": [
          "A graph with two Surfaces resolves each one independently at the same occupancy",
          "A graph with one Surface (or legacy roles) resolves exactly as before at every occupancy; tests/viewResolution.test.js, tests/graphViewPresentation.test.js, tests/semanticPresentation.test.js green",
          "Asking for a missing Surface returns the default Surface",
          "Switching Surface, resolution or focus leaves frame size, neighbor positions and the camera unchanged (test with reflowWorkspaceInstances and resolveWorkspaceCamera)",
          "The workspace camera targets and the renderer still agree on card size (workspaceCamera.js comment: they must use the same layout)"
        ],
