      "data": {
        "title": "1. How does a surface edge name its Surface, and what happens to declaration.surfaces[]?",
        "body": "A Surface needs an id (`defaultSurfaceId` already refers to one). Three places could carry it:\n(a) the `surface` edge itself (`edge.data.surfaceId`), so the relationship lives on the edge as the Declaration graph says roles do;\n(b) the existing `declaration.surfaces[]` entry, using its `viewNodeId` field as the chain head, with the edge derived from it;\n(c) the head View's `data.surfaceId`, which is what the Surface primitive graph currently proposes.\n\nRecommendation: (a). It matches \"the role lives on the Declaration's edge\", and it means membership comes from being in the chain, so Views need no `surfaceId` tag of their own. `declaration.surfaces[]` would keep only what an edge cannot say (label, `context`, and the `kind:'export'` entries that serve bridges, which this work must not disturb). Decide before task 1, and note it changes what the Surface primitive graph says.",
        "status": "resolved",
        "resolutionPath": "Decided: a stable id on the surface edge (edge.data.surfaceId); membership comes from the chain. Rename, delete, duplicate and default rules are in the goal's Decisions. Views need no surfaceId tag. declaration.surfaces[] keeps only kind:'export' entries for bridges.",
        "owner": "",
        "prominence": "high",
        "_classBinding": {
