      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "goal": "A Declaration exposes one repeatable surface port; each Surface is a View chain ending in a Glyph; and the Declaration resolves views like any class or template node",
        "status": "open",
        "taskProgress": {
          "completed": 6,
          "total": 12
        },
        "questionProgress": {
          "answered": 8,
          "total": 8
        },
        "taskTitles": [
          "1. Grammar: one repeatable surface relationship",
          "2. Edge classes and the Glyph port (content repo)",
          "3. One chain resolver, per Surface",
          "4. One glyph resolver: the end of the chain",
          "5. Scope the resolution ladder to a Surface",
          "6. Validator and defaults",
          "7. Route the Declaration through view resolution",
          "8. Authoring UI for Surfaces and chains",
          "9. Legacy graphs: read-compat, optional migration",
          "10. Smoke test: two Surfaces, bad chains",
          "11. Web view and 3D view as Surfaces",
          "12. Docs, LLM prompt, and the Surface primitive"
        ],
        "questionTitles": [
          "1. How does a surface edge name its Surface, and what happens to declaration.surfaces[]?",
          "2. What does a host do with a badly ordered chain?",
          "3. Legacy graphs: read-compat only, or also a migration script?",
          "4. Do Port nodes move to Surfaces too?",
          "5. Where does the Declaration's default identity-card View live?",
          "6. How does a host say which Surface it wants, and do web and 3D map one-to-one?",
          "7. Can a graph expose only a Glyph, with no View?",
          "8. How does an author give the Declaration node its own Views?"
        ],
        "taskListText": "1. Grammar: one repeatable surface relationship\n2. Edge classes and the Glyph port (content repo)\n3. One chain resolver, per Surface\n4. One glyph resolver: the end of the chain\n5. Scope the resolution ladder to a Surface\n6. Validator and defaults\n7. Route the Declaration through view resolution\n8. Authoring UI for Surfaces and chains\n9. Legacy graphs: read-compat, optional migration\n10. Smoke test: two Surfaces, bad chains\n11. Web view and 3D view as Surfaces\n12. Docs, LLM prompt, and the Surface primitive",
        "questionListText": "1. How does a surface edge name its Surface, and what happens to declaration.surfaces[]?\n2. What does a host do with a badly ordered chain?\n3. Legacy graphs: read-compat only, or also a migration script?\n4. Do Port nodes move to Surfaces too?\n5. Where does the Declaration's default identity-card View live?\n6. How does a host say which Surface it wants, and do web and 3D map one-to-one?\n7. Can a graph expose only a Glyph, with no View?\n8. How does an author give the Declaration node its own Views?",
        "summarySource": "node-updater",
